00:10:55.640 --> 00:11:00.440
That always points to the latest
particular Docker Container.

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:01.970
And the reason why
I stopped here,

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:03.920
I want to speak
about the problem

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.580
with using image family or
Docker Containers' tags.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:11.150
The thing is, if you're
always pointing at the latest,

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:13.670
there are chances that
something like this

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:15.920
will come up eventually.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:19.040
This is NumPy community,
that they're basically

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:22.160
broadcasting to the world that
they're releasing a new NumPy.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:27.020
And this new NumPy has
introduced a lot of, a lot of,

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:28.130
a lot of cleanups.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:30.500
And this may impact
a lot of customers.

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:32.000
And I actually do
know customers who

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.340
got impacted because
of this updated NumPy,

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:36.590
precisely this one.

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:38.120
If you're using
family, or if you're

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:41.750
using Docker Container latest,
probably the latest update

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:45.620
already includes this NumPy
because of the security fixes

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:47.240
that this NumPy has.

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:50.450
And it probably will break you.

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:54.370
So if you're using either
Docker or our images,

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:57.740
you probably need to switch
from using family directly

00:11:57.740 --> 00:12:01.270
on your production to using
the following pipeline.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:02.120
It's three steps.

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:04.220
You're getting the latest
image from the family.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.420
You're testing it
with your solution.

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:08.450
And then you're
using it in prod.

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:12.870
Always like this-- please,
never use family directly

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:15.400
if you're using it
on the production.

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:17.810
Production doesn't mean
something that serves traffic.

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:21.220
For me today production is my
demo that I'm going to show.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:24.220
And for my demo, I'm going to
use a particular exact image.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:26.420
There is a command that
allows you to extract

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:28.040
exactly the image name.

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:30.650
And for today I'm going to
use TensorFlow latest CPU

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:32.690
and exact hash.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:35.210
This is the image that
basically I'm going to use.

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:37.340
And now I am switching
my command from family

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:39.200
to actual name.

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:42.170
Second one-- Image project.

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:43.724
We have some customers
who actually,

00:12:43.724 --> 00:12:45.140
when they're
creating an instance,

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:47.580
are setting projects
to their project.

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:52.670
But this is a project that
hosts the images, which is ours.

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:53.720
Maintain policy.

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:56.960
The thing is, if
you're using GPU,

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:01.050
your instance cannot be live
migrated to another hardware

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:01.550
machine.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:02.255
This is very important.

00:13:02.255 --> 00:13:04.670
And you need to know this
in taking it into account.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:06.870
If you're using GPU in
case of maintenance,

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:09.470
the only way is to actually
terminate the instance.

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:11.910
The next part, if you want
to create an instance,

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:15.230
you're setting up how or
what that accelerator is then

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:17.030
that you want to use.

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:19.800
This is the important
part, because you

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:22.950
need to not only choose
a particular accelerator

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:24.510
from the four that we have.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:27.930
You need to educate yourself
about the regions that

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:29.130
exist there.

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:30.690
If you're specifying
the wrong region

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:33.510
where an accelerator doesn't
exist, it will not work.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:36.000
And after setting
the correct region,

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:37.710
the correct
accelerator, you need

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:40.290
to set up the correct
amount of accelerator

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:42.160
that you will attach
to the instance.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.170
If the amount does
not exist, again,

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:46.915
you cannot create the
instance with, for instance,

00:13:46.915 --> 00:13:51.510
a tesla v100, and
four tesla v100s.

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:56.430
We do not support this
configuration yet.

00:13:56.430 --> 00:13:57.860
And the last one--

00:13:57.860 --> 00:14:01.200
this part is basically
giving permission to Google

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:04.650
on your behalf to install
the Nvidia driver.

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:08.270
The thing is, images come with
pre-installed CUDA, CuDNN.

00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:10.310
Everything's pre-set
up and tested,

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:12.190
except the Nvidia driver.

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:15.780
There are some reasons why
it has been done this way.

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:19.590
I am not going to dive into
that particular reason.

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:23.970
But without this flag you will
not have the Nvidia driver

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:25.600
on the instance installed.

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:29.190
Now, what else can you tune?

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:31.430
You can set the preemptible
flag if you need.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:33.495
Preemptible is basically--

00:14:33.495 --> 00:14:38.430
Who knows the concept of
preemptible instances?

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:39.180
OK.

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.340
So Google Compute
Engine had this concept

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:44.250
of preemptible instances,
where you are actually buying

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:46.920
the same instance, but
for the cheaper price,

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:50.550
with only one downside.

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:56.680
This instance can be switched
off at any point of time.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:57.780
So yes.

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:00.385
I personally, any time that I'm
writing preemptible instances,

00:15:00.385 --> 00:15:02.190
and then training a
lot of models there,

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:04.842
but from time to time, it might
be switched off without any--

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.710
how do you say it?

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:12.450
Anyway.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:15.284
Now, keep in mind that
you have a quota when

00:15:15.284 --> 00:15:16.200
you're creating a GPU.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.660
So you need to request a quota.

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:20.250
Quota for preemptible instances
are not equal to quota

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:21.750
to normal instances.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:25.590
If you have a quota
for Nvidia P100,

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:28.860
you do need to have a quota
for a preemptible Nvidia P100s

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:31.140
in order to start
preemptible instances.

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:33.790
Next, if you're going to
run the command like this,

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:36.660
you will get the smallest
possible instance.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:41.080
I think this guy has one core
and less than 1 GB memory.

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:46.590
So if you want you can attach
any supported machine type.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:52.980
Next, you have the ability
to set up a type of the disk.

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:54.690
This is actually SSD.

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:59.940
We do have support of local SSD
disk, which are way faster--

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:00.810
SCSI disk.

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:03.650
This is basically some of the
measurements and the speed

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:06.750
that we can guarantee you
with the local SSD disk.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:10.680
And most importantly, we
have introduced a new type

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:15.510
of local SSD disk that
is actually using an NVMI

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.640
connection to the instance.

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:19.740
And it gives you--

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:23.520
so the one on the top with the
old one, the one on the bottom

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:24.710
the new one--

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:28.090
I wouldn't say twice faster,
but significantly faster,

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:29.770
which is important
if you are training

00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:33.510
something that requires
reading data from the disk.

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:34.440
And the last one.

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:35.350
Scopes.

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:38.640
These scopes are
actually required

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:40.830
if you want to use TPUs.

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:44.450
And here I'm actually
going to switch for just

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:47.950
a second to the console.

00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:49.517
I'm going to create a TPU node.

00:16:49.517 --> 00:16:51.600
I'm going to create a TPU
node because we're going

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:54.580
to use it in several minutes.

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:57.690
And since it takes to create
a TPU node several minutes,

00:16:57.690 --> 00:17:00.840
let me just start my
TPU node right now.

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:08.660
And let me press set up a
network, and press Create.

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:14.565
Now, when you are specifying
these metadata that I just

00:17:14.565 --> 00:17:16.440
mentioned, installing
Nvidia drivers through,

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:17.900
you're giving
permission for Google

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:20.760
to install a driver for you.

00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:25.960
And a lot of the customers
who actually have a cluster

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:27.750
with many machines
in the cluster,

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:30.830
they are kind of critical
for the time to boot.

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:33.920
If you have four machines, each
of the four machines has eight

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:37.040
worth of V100s, and you have
time to boot equal to three

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:39.350
minutes, you're already
paying for 12 minutes-- yeah,

00:17:39.350 --> 00:17:40.550
four machines--

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:44.870
12 minutes just for boot time.

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:46.610
So one of the common
questions is, OK,

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:49.370
how can I reduce time of
the boot for my machine?

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:51.210
And the answer is simple.

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.635
We are getting the image that
you want to create instances

00:17:54.635 --> 00:17:57.140
from from the Google Cloud.

00:17:57.140 --> 00:18:00.320
We can create a deep
learning instance.

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:02.224
We're waiting until the
driver is installed--

00:18:02.224 --> 00:18:03.140
three to five minutes.

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:03.994
It's horrible.

00:18:03.994 --> 00:18:04.910
We're working on this.

00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:06.610
And this will be improved--

00:18:06.610 --> 00:18:09.810
stopping the instance, and
creating our own deep learning

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:11.630
image out of that instance.

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:13.850
That simple, five steps and
you have your own image.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:18.550
And that image will have this
famous 15-second startup time

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:21.530
that GCE gives you
in other images.

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:22.030
OK.

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:23.030
Now, the main part.

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:26.942
Let's actually see
how we can use it.

00:18:26.942 --> 00:18:28.400
First, I will show
you Jupyter Lab.

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:32.270
Who have used here Jupyter
Notebooks or Jupyter Lab?

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:33.050
Nice.

00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:35.350
So the most important
part is Jupyter Lab.

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:40.590
So let's actually create
the instance and use it.

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:43.040
So I have copy,
pasted one command.

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:47.180
Exactly the command that I just
just showed you on the slides.

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:50.930
Let me make it bigger, bigger,
bigger, bigger, bigger.

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:53.240
So I have my image.

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:54.380
I have the instance name.

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:56.010
I have zone.

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:58.220
Let's go and create it.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:18:59.030
Let's wait.

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:02.150
It should take maybe 20
seconds or half a minute max,

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:03.720
because this is a CPU instance.

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:10.657
It's not going to install
the Nvidia driver.

00:19:10.657 --> 00:19:12.740
This is a command that I
originally showed to you.

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:17.180
This part is not required
if you are not using GPUs.

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:19.850
So basically this
is the end command.

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:20.660
OK.

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:22.530
Let's see if the
instance showed up.

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:23.030
Nope.

00:19:23.030 --> 00:19:26.246
Usually it's faster,
30 or 40 seconds.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:30.110
OK.

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:34.770
While we are waiting, this
is a small improvement,

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:37.320
one of the commands that I
mentioned to you on my GitHub.

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:40.180
A command that allows you
to SSH to the instance.

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:42.080
It's just slightly
nicer to the, say,

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:44.840
gssh instead of
gcloud compute ssh

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:46.480
and then the name
of the instance.

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:48.050
Just a small
improvement that you

00:19:48.050 --> 00:19:49.940
will find on the GitHub page.

00:19:54.071 --> 00:19:55.570
In order to connect
to the instance,

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:58.430
you will have to run this gssh
INSTANCE_NAME, specify zone

00:19:58.430 --> 00:19:59.930
and the port mapping.

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:01.880
This is the only
one thing that you

00:20:01.880 --> 00:20:04.970
need to do after your
instance is up in order

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:07.520
to start using Jupyter Lab.

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:08.600
Instance is up.

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:11.690
So let's actually do it.

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:13.880
Let me copy, paste my command.

00:20:17.860 --> 00:20:21.410
We're connecting
to the images live.

00:20:21.410 --> 00:20:23.550
Let's go to the browser.

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:27.990
Let's open local host, 8080.

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:29.610
And here is Jupyter.

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:32.830
You have a Jupyter if you would
create the instance with GPU.

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:34.770
You will have GPU with V100.

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:37.080
You literally don't
need to do anything else

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:39.180
except start your instance.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:42.210
We're already spinning
up the Jupyter

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:46.110
in the background that is
listening on the port 8080.

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:48.270
The only thing that
is left to do for you

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:52.020
is actually to
open your browser.

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:54.460
That's literally it.

00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:59.180
Now, next use case.

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:02.530
The next use case is the Colab.

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:05.600
Who have used Colab?

00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:06.450
OK.

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:11.430
So for those of you who have
not, let me show what Colab is.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:14.100
Colab is the Google
Service, that basically

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:16.350
is a notebook in the cloud.

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:18.960
It's very similar
to the notebooks.

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:22.350
It actually provides
a similar use case,

00:21:22.350 --> 00:21:23.800
but it's completely hosted.

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:26.130
So you don't have
a machine behind.

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:28.920
But this creates
some implications.

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:32.270
Let me show you exactly
the implications.

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:34.020
So if you have a
Google Cloud project,

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:36.810
and you have your machine,
you have TPU inside,

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:39.480
and you want to use it
with the Colab, you cannot,

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:45.291
because the Colab doesn't have
any notion about Google Cloud

00:21:45.291 --> 00:21:45.790
project.

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:49.260
It does not understand that you
have a Google Cloud project.

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:52.200
It cannot connect to
any resources there.

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:54.690
However, you do have a laptop.

00:21:54.690 --> 00:21:56.730
And your laptop does
have a connection

00:21:56.730 --> 00:21:59.250
to your instance with
port mapping 8080

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:02.120
that we just saw,
precisely for the reason

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:03.720
of opening Jupyter Lab.

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:06.970
And your instances do
have a connection to TPU.

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:09.300
So now the last
part that is missing

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:12.590
is to connect Colab to
your laptop on port 8080.

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:14.790
And this is exactly
the functionality

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:17.880
that we have released
several weeks ago.

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:22.320
We now have the ability to
use our deep learning images

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:24.420
as a back end for the Colab.

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:27.730
And it works very, very simply.

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:29.630
Let me go back to the Colab.

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:31.290
So this is an official Colab.

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:34.920
Let me increase the font.

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:37.260
Let me double check that
this is official Colab.

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:39.600
Let's see the version
of the TensorFlow

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:41.040
that's actually installed here.

00:22:44.310 --> 00:22:45.510
It's doing something.

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:47.820
And I hope it
eventually will work.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:49.530
It should be 1.9.

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:53.610
Yes, it's 1.9, because it's the
official version of the cloud.

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:57.150
Now, let me press Connect
to the Local Runtime.

00:22:57.150 --> 00:22:59.680
Basically what this will
do is it will connect Colab

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:02.190
to my local runtime.

00:23:02.190 --> 00:23:04.440
And the port is 8080.

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:07.230
And my machine right
now on the port 8080

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:10.920
has a mirror of the port
8080 of the deep learning VM

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:12.390
instance that we had.

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:14.330
Let me press Connect.

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:15.030
That's it.

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:18.720
Now the Colab is
using remote machine.

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:24.150
And, therefore, we will have
TensorFlow 1.9 that we just

00:23:24.150 --> 00:23:27.940
have released in our
VM images yesterday,

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:29.650
together with TensorFlow 1.10.

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:31.560
So yes, 1.10, by the
way, got released,

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:35.450
if you haven't been
following on the news.

00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:37.890
This basically means,
so now you can use Colab

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:39.300
with anything you
want, including

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:44.274
PyTorch, any possible
GPU, and even TPUs.

00:23:44.274 --> 00:23:46.440
Since, as I said, now that
we're using deep learning

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:50.600
image as a back end, let me
create a brand new notebook

00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:51.990
in the Colab.

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:57.710
And just to show that we do
have a TPU, access to the TPU,

00:23:57.710 --> 00:24:00.020
I will run one cell.

00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:04.000
And we can run it here.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:08.650
Only one cell, that
will connect to the TPU.

00:24:08.650 --> 00:24:12.010
Let me first get my IP address.

00:24:12.010 --> 00:24:14.660
Oh, the same, 10.0.101.

00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:15.160
Yes.

00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:16.450
It's the same.

00:24:16.450 --> 00:24:17.140
Yes.

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:19.130
It's actually connected.

00:24:19.130 --> 00:24:19.890
So it works.

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:22.294
So now you can use
Colab with the TPU.

00:24:22.294 --> 00:24:23.710
As I said, you can
do whatever you

00:24:23.710 --> 00:24:27.160
want in Colab with deep
learning images, PyTorch,

00:24:27.160 --> 00:24:31.150
any possible GPUs.

00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:34.060
Now, another question
that we usually

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:37.230
have from the customers
for the VM images

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:39.232
is how do I do Anaconda?

00:24:39.232 --> 00:24:40.190
I want to use Anaconda.

00:24:40.190 --> 00:24:42.110
And this is nice.

00:24:42.110 --> 00:24:44.590
The Anaconda scenarios
have some problems.

00:24:44.590 --> 00:24:47.610
And the reason for this problem,
if you install Anaconda,

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:51.280
if you're creating a new virtual
environment inside of Anaconda,

00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:55.619
you need to install TensorFlow
within that environment.

00:24:55.619 --> 00:24:56.410
How do you do this?

00:24:56.410 --> 00:24:58.720
If you're going to install
official TensorFlow,

00:24:58.720 --> 00:25:01.540
it will not work, because
official TensorFlow requires

00:25:01.540 --> 00:25:05.950
CUDA 9.0, and we have 9.2.

00:25:05.950 --> 00:25:08.290
In order to support
this, we are actually

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:13.240
providing a binary, our Google
Compute Engine optimized binary

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:15.760
that are pre-baked
inside of the image.

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:20.740
So you will have the
ability to install them.

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:22.150
So this is a small
script that we

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:23.905
do have in the official
documentation that

00:25:23.905 --> 00:25:27.460
are basically citing
the path where

00:25:27.460 --> 00:25:29.020
we're pre-baking the binary.

00:25:29.020 --> 00:25:32.230
And then you just can run deep
install inside of your Anaconda

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:34.850
environment.

00:25:34.850 --> 00:25:35.830
OK.

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:40.450
Next, how you can get
help if you're stuck.

00:25:40.450 --> 00:25:44.020
We have many different
channels to support you guys.

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:45.960
We do have official
docs, of course.

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:49.270
But if you want
something else, I

00:25:49.270 --> 00:25:52.930
have several blogs where I'm
actively adding different use

00:25:52.930 --> 00:25:54.790
cases for our images.

00:25:54.790 --> 00:25:57.880
We do have a dedicated
StackOverflow tag, which

00:25:57.880 --> 00:26:00.340
we are actively monitoring.

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:02.470
Please do submit
questions there.

00:26:02.470 --> 00:26:04.930
We do have a pretty
active Google Group

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:07.180
with different users
who might help you,

00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:10.930
even before I or my colleague
will spot the question.

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:13.540
You can always ping me,
even on personal Twitter.

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:15.770
I would really
appreciate any questions.

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:19.000
You can send me a
mail, and I will answer

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:21.850
any possible ways for you guys.

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:22.970
OK.

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:26.210
In short overview,
deep learning images

00:26:26.210 --> 00:26:28.960
is the pre-baked images
that have everything

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:32.780
ready to start with different
ideas, Jupyter Lab, Jupyter

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:38.260
Notebook, Colab back end,
and almost any framework

00:26:38.260 --> 00:26:42.080
that you can imagine.

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:46.630
Now, this is the survey, which
is really, really important.

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:49.030
If you can, guys,
help us to understand

00:26:49.030 --> 00:26:53.170
what you want to see in the
future version of the VM

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:58.090
images, what you want us to
add as a unit test, which

00:26:58.090 --> 00:27:00.880
case scenario you want
us to support, please

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:03.190
do participate in this survey.

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:03.790
Thank you.

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:07.990
Then this is all
from my side, guys.

00:27:07.990 --> 00:27:09.190
Thank you for coming.

00:27:09.190 --> 00:27:11.770
I hope there is more
pizza still left.

00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:14.663
And I hope to see you next time.

